Cell["Limits and holonomy", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b03dc29a-4e2c-4d6d-9807-725199749de7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A continuum claim needs a sequence of substrates, a metric scaling, \
comparison maps and a specified convergence of total spaces, bases and \
projections. The ray experiment establishes a stable finite direction bundle \
on one substrate. Proving a topological bundle limit also requires the \
relevant local product structure; see ",
 ButtonBox["Hatcher, Algebraic Topology, Section 4.2, pp. 376\[Dash]377",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"], None},
  ButtonNote->"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellDingbat->None,ExpressionUUID->"ebd9ca3b-43cb-4021-8348-66e7367796bc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Fiber scaling can obstruct such a limit. If every vertical fiber is one \
fixed connected graph of diameter ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["D", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "f9f8e78f-0480-48e1-8ede-ba69c330f87d"],
 " and every total edge has length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "21f8feec-9f06-4b87-a4da-c1c486849594"],
 ", distances within a fiber are at most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["D", "TI"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bf696ad4-d610-429f-ade0-359765ba15c2"],
 ". When ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
    RowBox[{"\[Rule]", "0"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"02265f81-8832-48a0-b3eb-b9effb24507d"],
 ", these fibers collapse in the total-space metric. A nontrivial limiting \
fiber therefore requires a different regime or additional structure."
}], "Text",
 CellDingbat->None,ExpressionUUID->"fded9430-1c08-4b67-ae23-a3914485ccaa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Holonomy already has an exact coordinate-invariance statement at finite \
scale. Under fiber-coordinate bijections ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["p", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2c4fb3ea-b137-4185-a931-b68802327bd5"],
 ", edge transport becomes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["q", "TI"]], 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["T", "TI"], 
     StyleBox[
      RowBox[{"p", "q"}], "TI"]], 
    SubsuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["p", "TI"], 
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "867756be-34d3-4814-8662-03ddbf596d28"],
 ". Intermediate factors cancel around a closed walk, so its holonomy becomes \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["p", "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["H", "TI"], 
    SubsuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["p", "TI"], 
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d997ed33-3707-4502-bd78-ba4ad6238365"],
 " and its conjugacy class is unchanged. The original tutorial attributes \
this finite gauge interpretation to Ioana Milea's Fibered Graph Gauge Theory \
Basics, unpublished, 2026. Curvature additionally requires chosen faces or a \
loop family and an appropriate scaling of their holonomy."
}], "Text",
 CellDingbat->None,ExpressionUUID->"34dfe187-ec3c-4715-8b82-4cc1cb109c90"],

